import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
import Picture from '../../components/Picture.astro';
import BlogSchema from '../../components/BlogSchema.astro';
import { blogSchemaBySlug } from '../../data/blog-schema';

const entry = blogSchemaBySlug['bra-pads-and-inserts-sourcing'];
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Bra Pads and Inserts: Sourcing the Part That Decides Your Shape"
  description="Foam grades, pad thickness by cup size, moulding versus die-cutting and how inserts move unit cost - a factory view of bra pad sourcing."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Bra Pads and Inserts</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-cream-dark mb-8">
      <Picture src="blog-bra-anatomy-hero.webp" alt="Internal cup layers and padding components laid out on a workbench at the Charlizi factory" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" priority sizes="(max-width: 768px) calc(100vw - 2rem), 704px" />
    </div>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">December 3, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">Bra Pads and Inserts: Sourcing the Part That Decides Your Shape</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        The pad is the cheapest component in the bra and the one that decides whether the
        bra sells twice. It costs between USD 0.06 and USD 0.55 depending on what it is
        made of, and it is the component most often specified by a single word: "push-up".
      </p>
      <p>
        That word covers at least four different products with different tooling, different
        failure modes and price points three times apart. This is how we break the category
        down when a buyer sends a tech pack, and the questions worth asking before the
        sampling PO goes out.
      </p>

      <h2>Four things buyers call "the pad"</h2>
      <p>
        Before quoting, we ask which one is actually meant. The answer changes the mould,
        the assembly sequence and the unit cost.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Moulded foam cup.</strong> The whole cup is a single moulded foam piece, usually polyurethane (PU) foam 2–6 mm thick at the apex, thicker at the base. This is the standard seamless and T-shirt bra build. The pad and the cup are the same object.</li>
        <li><strong>Cut-and-sew insert.</strong> A separate shaped pad dropped into a pocket or bonded between fabric layers of a cut-and-sew bra. Cheaper tooling because it is die-cut from sheet, not moulded.</li>
        <li><strong>Graduated push-up pad.</strong> Thickness varies across the pad, thickest at the lower outer quadrant, tapering to near zero at the top edge. This is what produces lift rather than volume.</li>
        <li><strong>Air or gel pocket.</strong> A sealed pocket of air, gel or silicone gel set into the lower cup. Highest unit cost, and the one with a distinct warranty problem - see the leakage section below.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A tech pack that says "push-up padding, 8 mm" and nothing else will be quoted four
        different ways by four factories, and none of them will match what you pictured.
        For how the pad integrates with the rest of the construction, the
        <a href="/blog/bra-anatomy">bra anatomy breakdown</a> covers all twelve components.
      </p>

      <h2>Foam grades and what each one is actually for</h2>
      <p>
        Polyurethane foam is not one material either. The differences that matter are
        density and cell structure, and both are quoted in numbers you can demand.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Standard PU foam, 25–35 kg/m³.</strong> The workhorse. Fine for T-shirt bras and low-lift moulded cups. Compresses noticeably after 40–60 washes and yellows under UV.</li>
        <li><strong>High-density PU, 40–55 kg/m³.</strong> Holds shape through more cycles, resists the crease that forms at the apex. Roughly 15–25% more per pad. Worth it on any bra above a C cup.</li>
        <li><strong>Memory foam (viscoelastic).</strong> Slow recovery, soft hand-feel. Popular for comfort positioning. Poor resilience at high temperature - it can go flat in a warehouse in a hot climate before it ever reaches the customer.</li>
        <li><strong>Spacer foam.</strong> Two knit faces with a spacer yarn between, so it breathes. Higher cost, and it is a fabric rather than a foam, which means it is cut and moulded differently.</li>
        <li><strong>Silicone gel.</strong> Used as a fill inside the pad rather than the pad itself. Heavy, realistic hand-feel, and the highest cost per unit in the category.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Ask for density in kg/m³, not "high quality foam". Density is measurable, comparable
        and the single number that best predicts how the pad looks after a year.
      </p>

      <h2>Thickness by size band, not one number for the range</h2>
      <p>
        The most common costing mistake we see is one pad spec across S to XL. It produces
        two failures at once: the small sizes look padded and artificial, and the large
        sizes are under-supported in exactly the quadrant that needs the most.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we run as a starting point, adjusted per cup shape:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>32–34 A/B:</strong> 4–6 mm at the apex, 8 mm at the base. Thin, mostly for opacity and nipple coverage.</li>
        <li><strong>34–36 B/C:</strong> 6–8 mm at the apex, 10–12 mm at the base. The volume band where most fashion push-ups sit.</li>
        <li><strong>36–38 C/D:</strong> 8 mm at the apex, 12–15 mm at the base, with a wider support footprint rather than a taller one.</li>
        <li><strong>40+ bands:</strong> shape and support over lift. A thick graduated pad on a large cup looks like a shelf and reads cheap. Most buyers move to a shape-focused mould here.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If your size range runs past D, the grading decisions upstream of the pad matter
        more than the pad itself - see the <a href="/blog/size-grading">size grading guide</a>
        for how cups are graded above D and where the wire and strap specs change.
      </p>

      <h2>Moulded or die-cut: the tooling trade-off</h2>
      <p>
        Both routes produce a pad. They differ in what you pay up front and what you pay
        per piece.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Moulded.</strong> Aluminium or steel tool, typically USD 1,200–3,500 per size for a cup mould. The pad comes out with a controlled thickness profile, a sealed surface and a defined edge, which is why moulded cups hold their shape. Sampling takes 20–30 days because the tool has to be cut.</li>
        <li><strong>Die-cut.</strong> A steel rule die costs USD 150–400 and arrives in a week. The pad is cut from sheet foam, so thickness is whatever the sheet is - no profile, no sealed edge, and a visible cut edge that crumbles after washing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Below roughly 500 pcs per size, die-cutting is the rational choice: you are not
        amortising a mould you may never reuse. Above that, the moulded pad is usually
        cheaper per unit and dramatically better in wear. The crossover point is worth
        calculating rather than assuming - it depends on how many sizes the range carries,
        because the tool cost multiplies per size and the unit saving does not.
      </p>

      <h2>Where pads fail in the field</h2>
      <p>
        Almost every padding complaint we see traces to one of four causes, and all four
        are decided at sourcing, not at manufacture.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Apex collapse.</strong> Low-density foam, or a pad moulded too thin at the apex to hold tension. Shows as a dent after a few dozen washes.</li>
        <li><strong>Edge crumbling.</strong> A die-cut edge on open-cell foam. Unsealed cells take water, dry out, and shed. A moulded sealed edge is the only real fix.</li>
        <li><strong>Pad migration.</strong> A loose insert that shifts inside the pocket and bunches at the side seam. Fixed at pattern stage by tacking, bonding or encapsulating the pad.</li>
        <li><strong>Gel pocket leakage.</strong> Seam failure in the gel fill, usually along the weld. Ask what the burst test pressure is and whether it is done 100% or by AQL sample.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The migration failure is worth a note because it does not appear in a fit session
        on a mannequin. It appears on a customer in week three. On adhesive bras the
        equivalent risk is migration through the adhesive layer - covered in the
        <a href="/blog/adhesive-bra-spec-guide">adhesive bra spec guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What pads cost, and what moves the number</h2>
      <p>
        Indicative ex-factory pad cost at 1,000 pcs, per pair:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Die-cut standard PU:</strong> USD 0.06–0.14</li>
        <li><strong>Moulded PU, standard density:</strong> USD 0.15–0.32</li>
        <li><strong>Moulded PU, high density, graduated:</strong> USD 0.28–0.55</li>
        <li><strong>Gel-filled pocket:</strong> USD 0.60–1.40</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Four levers move that number, in order of impact: gel versus foam, moulded versus
        die-cut, density, and whether the pad is wrapped in a fabric cover. Fabric covering
        the pad is easy to leave out of a quote and obvious in the sample - the pad has a
        visible seam line where the cover was closed.
      </p>

      <h2>The spec sheet we ask buyers to fill in</h2>
      <p>
        Six lines. With these, the quote is a number. Without them, it is a guess.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Pad type: moulded cup, insert, graduated, or gel pocket</li>
        <li>Foam density in kg/m³, or the fill medium if gel</li>
        <li>Thickness at apex and at base, per size band</li>
        <li>Moulded edge or cut edge</li>
        <li>Encapsulated during assembly, tacked, or loose in a pocket</li>
        <li>Wash cycles before failure is considered a defect, and the test standard</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The last line is the one that gets skipped and the one that decides who pays for
        returns. "Machine washable" is not a spec. Forty cycles at 40 °C with a defined
        pass criterion is.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the pad is part of a matched set, read
        <a href="/blog/bra-set-development">bra set development</a> for how pad, cup and
        panty rise have to be planned together. And if you are choosing a supplier on
        this basis, the <a href="/blog/choosing-bra-manufacturer">manufacturer selection
        guide</a> lists the factory questions that separate an assembler from a moulder.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Sourcing pads and inserts to your spec?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone mt-1">We cut pad tooling in-house, mould 25–55 kg/m³ PU foam, and quote thickness by size band rather than one number for the range. MOQ 200–300 pcs per design and colourway. <a href="/contact" class="text-accent hover:underline">Send your pad spec</a> or see the <a href="/products/bras" class="text-accent hover:underline">bra range</a>.</p>
    </div>
  <BlogSchema slug="bra-pads-and-inserts-sourcing" entry={entry} />

  </article>
</BlogLayout>
